WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:01.130 --> 00:00:06.640
The next group of operators to look at sequencing
for is the logical group of operators.

00:00:06.640 --> 00:00:10.430
Within this group, there is an order of operations
we need to make sure we understand.

00:00:10.430 --> 00:00:15.230
I will cover that part first, and then we
will slot this group of operators against

00:00:15.230 --> 00:00:19.930
the two types we have already covered: assignment
and comparison.

00:00:19.930 --> 00:00:24.960
I have a code example in this 133-logical
file for a logical game situation where one

00:00:24.960 --> 00:00:30.890
needs to unlock a level and make a target
score to get a message that a goal has been

00:00:30.890 --> 00:00:32.000
met.

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:37.680
There are three logical keywords in use in
Python: not, and, and or.

00:00:37.680 --> 00:00:40.020
Take a look at the if statement on line 4.

00:00:40.020 --> 00:00:45.340
We will cover if statements elsewhere in the
course but focus on the logical keywords here.

00:00:45.340 --> 00:00:53.431
If they have not unlocked a level or their
score is less than the target score, they

00:00:53.431 --> 00:00:57.640
will see a message that they still have goals
to make.

00:00:57.640 --> 00:01:01.699
Otherwise, they will see that the goals are
met.

00:01:01.699 --> 00:01:05.790
In order of operations, not always comes before
and.

00:01:05.790 --> 00:01:08.280
And comes before or.

00:01:08.280 --> 00:01:09.960
Why is that important?

00:01:09.960 --> 00:01:11.890
Think of it this way.

00:01:11.890 --> 00:01:20.890
We need to know whether a condition is not
something before we can compare it with other

00:01:20.890 --> 00:01:22.940
conditions.

00:01:22.940 --> 00:01:29.860
In this context, we have to know if they unlocked
a level before we can compare it to the condition

00:01:29.860 --> 00:01:32.390
of the score.

00:01:32.390 --> 00:01:39.000
For the test variables on lines 1 and 2, we
see that they unlocked a level, but the score

00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:43.939
is less than the target score, so the goals
have not been met.

00:01:43.939 --> 00:01:50.190
When we run this code, that’s the message
we get.

00:01:50.190 --> 00:02:00.820
To see the importance of not, I will reverse
the criteria in the if statement.

00:02:00.820 --> 00:02:07.390
Upon rerunning the code, we get the same result.

00:02:07.390 --> 00:02:14.690
Not takes precedence because it only needs
to look at one value and deem it true or false.

00:02:14.690 --> 00:02:21.540
A logical keyword such as and or or needs
two values to compare.

00:02:21.540 --> 00:02:27.180
In the order of operator precedence, comparison
comes before logical, and logical comes before

00:02:27.180 --> 00:02:31.790
assignment since assignment is last in the
order of preferences and not any part of any

00:02:31.790 --> 00:02:33.529
comparison or logical statement.

